  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates the variance of each lottery and prefers the one with smaller variance. Variance can be calculated from the outcomes and their probabilities. For Lottery A: outcomes are 6000 (25%) and 0 (75%). The expected value is 0.25×6000 + 0.75×0 = 1500. Variance = 0.25×(6000−1500)² + 0.75×(0−1500)² = 0.25×(4500)² + 0.75×(−1500)² = 0.25×20,250,000 + 0.75×2,250,000 = 5,062,500 + 1,687,500 = 6,750,000. For Lottery B: outcomes are 4000 (25%), 2000 (25%), 0 (50%). Expected value is 0.25×4000 + 0.25×2000 + 0.5×0 = 1000 + 500 = 1500. Variance = 0.25×(4000−1500)² + 0.25×(2000−1500)² + 0.5×(0−1500)² = 0.25×(2500)² + 0.25×(500)² + 0.5×(−1500)² = 0.25×6,250,000 + 0.25×250,000 + 0.5×2,250,000 = 1,562,500 + 62,500 + 1,125,000 = 2,750,000. Thus, Lottery B has a lower variance (2,750,000) than Lottery A (6,750,000). The decision reason is applicable because variance can be computed from the given outcomes and probabilities, and it clearly prefers Lottery B due to its lower variance.",
